<h2 id="eligible-vehicles">Choosing an Eligible Vehicle</h2>

<p>State programs set their own vehicle rules, usually an MSRP cap, a minimum electric range, and a requirement that you buy or lease from an in-state dealer. Check the program's eligible-vehicle list before you sign, and ask the dealer whether the rebate is applied at the point of sale or claimed afterward. Before buying a used EV, run its VIN through our <a href="/">free VIN decoder and recall check</a>.</p>

<h2 id="how-to-claim">How to Claim EV Incentives</h2>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Check the program is open.</strong> Many state rebates run in funding rounds and pause when money runs out.</li>
  <li><strong>Confirm the vehicle and price qualify</strong> on the official eligible-vehicle list.</li>
  <li><strong>Apply in the right order.</strong> Some programs require pre-approval before you buy (especially income-qualified ones); others are applied by the dealer at the point of sale or claimed within a set number of days after purchase.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep your paperwork:</strong> purchase or lease contract, registration and proof of residency.</li>
  <li><strong>Add your utility's rebate.</strong> Utilities often pay toward a Level 2 charger and offer cheaper off-peak charging rates.</li>
</ol>

